[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-32167"] :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and III give us true statements:\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $det(A)\\ne 0.$* $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-47653"] :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- The lines $6x+2y=1$ and $-3x+ky=-2$ intersect at infinitely many points" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6\\cdot k-2\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6k+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $6k+6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -1.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, there must be a unique zero solution to $Ax=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement I is true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection/a19a3eba96c781690660fad5de2eee069b1182f1e4546e2dd87f2704100295c6" :answer/id #uuid "bca0fdcf-aa0b-5275-8400-08e14037c3b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection/08ee2a4b69e18998ff0a7e6ce0d066315481ef21af58328fbfed57b1814231ac" :answer/id #uuid "11e2ddf0-f804-5a30-980d-66304828d094" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection/2a71f4367b96c8d0dda1e046ee1e634439b2c69d76f4f7f4da63c2ecbfa8fe21" :answer/id #uuid "5be870e4-e9bd-5937-987a-d79308c5cc3a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has no solution" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection/7a02063a47040bd0e0b369cdc8d609dcb4b574da28f31866b520751bc4e0738e" :answer/id #uuid "81b9a644-f5fb-5579-a1e9-3121dea65ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution for the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection/51586bb89717d61033bbacf9acd3fca0fa108cdcc69178ae7f6583371470cf32" :answer/id #uuid "4a2b59cd-65d9-5d5c-8608-f9aa1b253dd0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ are parallel" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "266ea60b-4a4f-549a-9d0f-158bc22cdf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73608/selection/a19a3eba96c781690660fad5de2eee069b1182f1e4546e2dd87f2704100295c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-73608/selection/a19a3eba96c781690660fad5de2eee069b1182f1e4546e2dd87f2704100295c6" "q-73608/selection/08ee2a4b69e18998ff0a7e6ce0d066315481ef21af58328fbfed57b1814231ac" "q-73608/selection/2a71f4367b96c8d0dda1e046ee1e634439b2c69d76f4f7f4da63c2ecbfa8fe21" "q-73608/selection/7a02063a47040bd0e0b369cdc8d609dcb4b574da28f31866b520751bc4e0738e" "q-73608/selection/51586bb89717d61033bbacf9acd3fca0fa108cdcc69178ae7f6583371470cf32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73608" :question/id #uuid "0b78864d-df1c-54a9-a660-cac490a85eb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-73608" :question/problem "Consider the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-73608/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "055872c1-865f-5c07-a212-c3458a16c6e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3a5756ce-4709-5ad0-8ac0-336e04a07147" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "c2fadcc5-30ad-58f2-ae98-6868b696e959" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "23a222e3-0df3-59bd-845f-536818990655" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f8d94ba1-f6fb-565d-aa03-917dacfde257" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a723885-d517-5f98-9d23-ade68e8c13a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-128124/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-128124/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-128124/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-128124/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-128124/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-128124/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128124" :question/id #uuid "9c80544f-2eae-5edd-8bb7-5e25399a9d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-128124" :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-128124/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9923c6c3-e425-54ae-a533-09421d5578cb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128124"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "81c855a2-cc86-5577-9b7e-1ccc7c278a35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "6815e99b-39e5-5390-a7ff-a18b9ef9d82e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "6347f32d-ea1a-511a-ac3d-398ea00fac6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "87b920c0-c2c6-530f-9eeb-0037c3cb2c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "70dc487f-b422-5eda-bf9a-41933ac786a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfc6be7-d43c-53f2-8187-a2e87081d2d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-128151/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-128151/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-128151/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-128151/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-128151/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-128151/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-128151" :question/id #uuid "d3d3c32b-5e5b-571b-a2d8-3ef8f60e3dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-128151" :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ does not exist\n- The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-128151/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64b2036f-da0f-59de-8a39-6b33e6cbdd04"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-128151"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
